## Q5: Which customer segment is more profitable?



| Metric | B2B | B2C | Winner |
|--------|-----|-----|--------|
| Total orders | 9,163 | 9,077 | Draw |
| Avg order value | $1,432 | $435 | B2B |
| Total profit | $2.67M | $636K | B2B |
| Avg margin | 23.69% | 16.01% | B2B |
| Marketing/profit | 39.3% | 80.69% | B2B |
| Loss rate | 1.4% | 7.3% | B2B |
| Margin/discount unit | 1.47 | 1.65 | B2C |

**Score: B2B wins 8 out of 9 metrics**

**Key insight:** B2B generates 4.2× more profit than B2C
with nearly equal order volumes. B2C spends 80 cents
of marketing per $1 of profit — twice as expensive as B2B.

**Key nuance:** B2C loss rate is 5× higher than B2B.
Most of the B2C disadvantage comes from structural cost
issues, not from the segment itself.

**Recommendation:** Protect B2B as priority segment.
Audit B2C loss orders by category and channel.
Do not abandon B2C — optimise cost structure first.

 ## Q6: Which channel is most efficient after all costs?



| Channel | Margin | ROI | Loss Rate | Rank |
|---------|--------|-----|-----------|------|
| Wholesale | 26.48% | $3.22 | 0.5% | #1  |
| Distributor | 25.46% | $2.65 | 0.7% | #2  |
| Modern Trade | 19.91% | $1.42 | 2.7% | #3  |
| Online | 12.78% | $0.68 | 10.4% | #4  |

**All 4 channels are COGS-heavy (49–53%)**
This is a systemic business issue, not a channel issue.

**Critical finding — Online:**
Every $1 of Online marketing spend returns only $0.68
in profit — below the $1.00 break-even threshold.
Online loss rate (10.4%) is 20× higher than Wholesale.

**Wholesale + Distributor = 74.6% of total profit**
combined from roughly equal order volumes.

**Recommendation:** Zero promotional budget for Online.
Set minimum order value to offset 9.82% logistics burden.
Reallocate freed budget to Wholesale and Distributor.

*Statistically validated: ANOVA F=3,989, p≈0.00
confirms channel ROI differences are not random.*

## Q7: Do revenue peaks align with profit peaks?



**Key divergence — 2025 Q2:**
Highest gross sales of all 12 quarters (#1 by revenue)
but only #3 by profit. rank_gap = −2.

**Best quarter — 2024 Q4:**
#3 by revenue but #1 by profit ($297,694).
Low discount (12.79%) + strong margin (23.61%).

**Q2 pattern — consistent underperformance:**

| Year | Q2 QoQ delta | vs Q1 |
|------|-------------|-------|
| 2023 | −$14,997 | Low |
| 2024 | −$16,669 | Low |
| 2025 | −$842 | Low |

**Warning signal — 2025 Q4:**
Only quarter with negative YoY delta (−$11,765).
All other 11 quarters showed YoY growth.

**Key insight:** Discount levels are stable across
all quarters (12.67–13.24%). Profit variation is driven
by demand mix and channel mix — not by promotions.

**Recommendation:** Plan Q2 demand stimulation proactively.
Investigate 2025 Q4 decline before 2026 planning.
Replace gross revenue with margin-adjusted profit
as the primary quarterly KPI.
## Q8: When should each category be promoted?



**Categories do NOT peak simultaneously.**

| Category | Peak Months | Low Season | Safe for Promo |
|----------|------------|------------|----------------|
| Personal Care | Jan, Dec | Feb, Jul | Jan, May–Jun, Aug |
| Household | Apr–May, Jan | Jun–Jul | Apr, May, Dec |
| Dairy | Jan, Mar | Apr, Nov | Feb, Mar, Dec |
| Snacks | Nov→Dec* | Jun–Jul | Dec, Oct |
| Beverages | Aug | Feb–Mar | None safe 🔴 |

*Snacks seasonality shifted from Oct–Nov (2023–2024)
to Jan–Dec (2025) -potential consumer behaviour change.

**Beverages has no safe promotional window:**
Every peak month is classified as
"Risky— high volume low margin."
Running promotions during peaks compounds the problem.

**December = universal strong month:**
4 out of 5 categories are Above average in December.
Exception: Personal Care in December = Risky.
Promote Snacks, Household, Dairy, Beverages in December.
Use premium placement (not discounts) for Personal Care.

**Recommendation:** Build a category-specific
promotional calendar. Never run cross-category
promotions simultaneously - peaks are staggered
and one-size-fits-all campaigns destroy margin.
